  "body": "What happens: since #938 (PR #973) a pose within `UPRIGHT_TILT_RAD` (1e-9 rad) is judged upright, but just above it the reach laws switch to the tilted (enclosing) reading, which lowers each body band's bottom by its whole radius. An r12 holder over a Ø8 cutter beside a wall is clean at 1e-9 rad and convicted as `ShankHit{part:true}` at 2e-9 rad or 0.01 deg (review of PR #973, scratch check on the `beside_wall` fixture of `crates/app/tests/tool_body_bands.rs`). The verdict is conservative (a false collision, never a missed one), but any small commanded tilt pays it.\n\nExpected: the tilted enclosure dips a band by R·sin θ (the exact rim dip), so the verdict changes continuously with the tilt instead of jumping at the tolerance.\n\nEvidence: `crates/sim/src/verify/reach.rs` law (c)/(c3) tilted branch; `docs/decisions/938-tilt-tolerance.md` names this \"exact dip\" option as dropped from #938's scope.\n\nDone means: the tilted reading of a body band encloses the band by its exact R·sin θ dip (never less), pinned both ways - a real tilt that brings the rim into the part still convicts, and a 0.01 deg tilt of the #938 holder fixture no longer does.\n\nPart of the follow-up of #938.\n",
